      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights to achieve the same total weight as he does when lifting two 20-pound weights 12 times, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total weight lifted in one workout with two 20-pound weights:**\n   \\[\n   12 \\text{ lifts} \\times 2 \\text{ pounds per lift} = 24 \\text{ pounds}\n   \\]\n   So, lifting two 20-pound weights 12 times results in lifting 24 pounds in total.\n\n2. **Determine the total weight lifted with one 15-pound weight:**\n   Since one 20-pound weight equals two 15-pound weights, we can distribute four 15-pound weights to achieve the same weight as two 20-pound weights:\n   \\[\n   2 \\text{ 20-pound weights} = 4 \\text{ 15-pound weights}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total weight lifted in one workout with four 15-pound weights:**\n   \\[\n   12 \\text{ lifts} \\times 4 \\text{ pounds per lift} = 48 \\text{ pounds}\n   \\]\n   So, lifting four 15-pound weights 12 times results in lifting 48 pounds in total.\n\nThus, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{12}\\) times to achieve the same total weight as lifting the two 20-pound weights 12 times.",
