      "text": "To find out how many times Terrell must lift the 20-pound weights, we start by calculating the total weight lifted in one set of 12 repetitions. The total weight lifted per set is:\n\\[\n20 \\text{ pounds/weight} \\times 2 \\text{ weights} = 40 \\text{ pounds per set}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine how many sets of 12 repetitions Terrell must perform to lift the same total weight of 40 pounds. This can be set up as the following equation:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of repetitions} \\times 40 \\text{ pounds} = 40 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nSolving for the number of repetitions, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of repetitions} = \\frac{40 \\text{ pounds}}{40 \\text{ pounds/set}} \\times 12 \\text{ repetitions} = 1.6 \\text{ repetitions}\n\\]\n\nSince Terrell cannot lift weights in fractional repetitions, we interpret this calculation to mean that Terrell needs to lift the weights 15 times to get the exact total weight. Therefore, the number of times Terrell must lift the 20-pound weights is 15. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]\n\nTwo lines $\\rho_{1}: 9x-12y+12=0$ and $\\rho_{2}: x-3y+m=0$. If $\\rho_{2}$ is tangent to $\\rho_{1}$, then $m$ equals \n(A) 5 \n(B) 7 \n(C) -5 \n(D) -7\nTo determine the value of \\( m \\) for which the line \\(\\rho_2: x - 3y + m = 0\\) is tangent to the line \\(\\rho_1: 9x - 12y + 12 = 0\\), we need to find the condition under which the distance from the center of the first line to the second line is equal to the perpendicular distance from the center of the first line to the line itself.\n\nLet's follow the steps in detail:\n\n1. **Identify the center and perpendicular distance of \\(\\rho_1\\):**\n\n   The first line \\(\\rho_1: 9x - 12y + 12 = 0\\) can be rewritten in the standard form \\(Ax + By + C = 0\\):\n   \\[\n   9x - 12y + 12 = 0\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(A = 9\\), \\(B = -12\\), and \\(C = 12\\).\n\n   The center of \\(\\rho_1\\) is \\((0, 0)\\).\n\n   The perpendicular distance from the center \\