      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the total weight Terrell lifts when he uses two 20-pound weights and then find out how many times this weight needs to be lifted using two 15-pound weights.\n\nFirst, calculate the total weight lifted with two 20-pound weights:\n- Two weights = 2 * 20 pounds = 40 pounds\n- Number of repetitions = 12 times\nSo, the total weight lifted with two 20-pound weights is:\n\\[ 40 \\text{ pounds} * 12 \\text{ repetitions} = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNext, determine how many times Terrell needs to lift two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight (480 pounds):\n- Weights = 2 * 15 pounds = 30 pounds\n- Let \\( x \\) be the number of repetitions required\nSo, the equation to solve is:\n\\[ 30x = 480 \\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), divide both sides by 30:\n\\[ x = \\frac{480}{30} = 16 \\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight.",
